{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised","id":1406563,"title":"Just read Cult of Ecstasy Revised","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-05T19:49:12-07:00","id":1406563,"title":"Just read Cult of Ecstasy Revised","content":"I liked it overall, but I didn\u2019t like how it made BDSM seem really bad. The only faction that practiced it, the Acharne, were all-male and REALLY unpleasant.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m gonna go back and reread my copy of M20 to see if this part of the Sahajiya has gotten more enlightened in the past 15 or 20 years. I like the general concept of Ecstatic mages and I wouldn\u2019t mind playing one sometime, but I have a Hermetic character I\u2019ll be playing first if we ever get our M20 game started.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T20:09:35-07:00","id":1406570,"title":"","content":"I've found that Mage has had a bad track record of arbitrarily sorting world esoteric and cultural traditions into &quot;good&quot; and &quot;evil&quot; columns. M20 had this same problem with putting veneration of Santa Muerte firmly in the &quot;evil&quot; column by making a Santa Muerte centric Nephandi cult.<br /> <br /> And let's not get started on how it took Old White Wolf several years before they realized it probably wasn't kosher to make all rural Chinese &quot;witchcraft&quot; evil, by making the <i>Wu Keng</i> their face. And that they should probably walk that back and make non-evil, non-infernalist Chinese shamans that players could use. (But not enough to give them a <b>name</b> in the book they were introduced in, no, we had to dig into the MET content to find that out; it's &quot;Wu Nung&quot;, for the record).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T20:10:48-07:00","id":1406571,"title":"","content":"Thank you for clarifying that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T21:01:31-07:00","id":1406575,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that particular take on that faction is wonky on a number of levels. Guide to the Traditions, in a brief blurb in the history chapter, gives a (unfortunately brief) take that more accurately presents real world S&amp;M/B&amp;D culture, as well as why members do sometimes go off the rails. My take on them in the head cannon/homebrew thread is largely based on that. <br /> I'm generally of the opinion that mages (and sorcerers, etc.) in the WoD should be a little unnerving to most people - sometimes even to each other - but that doesn't automatically translate to wicked or evil. (I'll try to say something more detailed and profound tomorrow when I'm not typing with an X-Box controller.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T21:46:03-07:00","id":1406577,"title":"","content":"IMO, the First Edition Cult of Ecstasy book was much better. There's a brief bit where the viewpoint character (who I liked a lot better than Lee Ann, sorry) does have a BDSM themed encounter and it's not judgmental at all. <br /> <br /> Revised CoE was a disappointment, I liked some of the expanded ideas for factions (and that they focused more on fictional Cultists as opposed to supposed historical ones), but it lacked a lot of the personality and joy of the original one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T21:55:21-07:00","id":1406578,"title":"","content":"To be fair, I think the original Cult of Ecstasy book may be the crown jewel of the game line as a whole, so even without Revised Edition's wider problems that sourcebook was almost certainly going to be inferior to its predecessor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-05T22:01:28-07:00","id":1406579,"title":"","content":"The Acharne are also pretty bad in the Tradition Book: Cult of Ecstasy from 1996, which is Mage 2e. The Acharne aren't nice people--and it really ties into them violating the Code of Anada. That's because they are hurting others who don't want to be hurt. Consent is a <i>fundamental </i>part of the Code of Ananda and the Acharne don't care about consent.<br /> <br /> While there's not an official mechanic for mages being odd per se unless you bring Resonance into being, I remember the Normality mechanic as a fan mechanic from many years ago, probably on Ander's Mage page. A web search found Anders Sandberg, the host of that page's take on Normality here:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> Some Thoughts about Arete and Normality </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Basically, the higher a mage gets in Arete, the more alien they become to Sleepers. it makes a lot of sense, as if you can just walk into a closet and emerge somewhere else in the world with Correspondence 4 or know what is likely to happen with Entropy 2/Time 2, you aren't going to be too normal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-06T03:00:15-07:00","id":1406605,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised?p=1406579#post1406579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Acharne are also pretty bad in the Tradition Book: Cult of Ecstasy from 1996, which is Mage 2e. The Acharne aren't nice people--and it really ties into them violating the Code of Anada. That's because they are hurting others who don't want to be hurt. Consent is a <i>fundamental </i>part of the Code of Ananda and the Acharne don't care about consent.<br /> <br /> While there's not an official mechanic for mages being odd per se unless you bring Resonance into being, I remember the Normality mechanic as a fan mechanic from many years ago, probably on Ander's Mage page. A web search found Anders Sandberg, the host of that page's take on Normality here:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> Some Thoughts about Arete and Normality </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm\" target=\"_blank\"> http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-arete.htm </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Basically, the higher a mage gets in Arete, the more alien they become to Sleepers. it makes a lot of sense, as if you can just walk into a closet and emerge somewhere else in the world with Correspondence 4 or know what is likely to happen with Entropy 2/Time 2, you aren't going to be too normal.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know. That\u2019s why I said they were really unpleasant. I just wish they weren\u2019t the only faction into the BDSM scene. But maybe I\u2019ll get the original CoE book and see if I like it better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-06T04:43:55-07:00","id":1406609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised#post1406563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I liked it overall, but I didn\u2019t like how it made BDSM seem really bad. The only faction that practiced it, the Acharne, were all-male and REALLY unpleasant.</div> </div> </div> </div>Though it doesn't refer to it by name, pg. 51 is pretty clearly discussing a form of mystical BDSM, and it's definitely there in the background. It seems fairly well implied that it's a common element in their practices, it just doesn't define any one faction.<br /> <br /> The Acharne on the other hand... I'm not sure I'd call what they do BDSM exactly. Their torture practices aren't about enjoyment, they're about developing the will to overcome pain. It's frankly more like cult brainwashing; I think there's a good reason their nickname is a reference to Fight Club.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've found that Mage has had a bad track record of arbitrarily sorting world esoteric and cultural traditions into &quot;good&quot; and &quot;evil&quot; columns. M20 had this same problem with putting veneration of Santa Muerte firmly in the &quot;evil&quot; column by making a Santa Muerte centric Nephandi cult. </div> </div> </div>Santa Muerte is explicitly referenced as a new element of the Euthanatos in M20. Los Sangritos never mentions Santa Muerte, and isn't really similar at all beyond being Mexican in origin.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-06T08:45:36-07:00","id":1406640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised?p=1406609#post1406609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Though it doesn't refer to it by name, pg. 51 is pretty clearly discussing a form of mystical BDSM, and it's definitely there in the background. It seems fairly well implied that it's a common element in their practices, it just doesn't define any one faction.<br /> <br /> The Acharne on the other hand... I'm not sure I'd call what they do BDSM exactly. Their torture practices aren't about enjoyment, they're about developing the will to overcome pain. It's frankly more like cult brainwashing; I think there's a good reason their nickname is a reference to Fight Club.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Santa Muerte is explicitly referenced as a new element of the Euthanatos in M20. Los Sangritos never mentions Santa Muerte, and isn't really similar at all beyond being Mexican in origin.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you. I just don\u2019t like the Acharne at all, though they would make good \u201cevil\u201d NPCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-06T20:36:57-07:00","id":1406722,"title":"","content":"So, I said that Mages should be at least a little unsettling to most mundane people, and that's certainly true for the Cult of Ecstasy, whose paradigm is built around breaking down barriers and expanding the limits of their perceptions. For the most part, that's about their own barriers, but they also try to push those of society as a whole. <br /> <br /> I think one of the major problems with the Acharne is one they share with the other two &quot;rebel&quot; factions, the Hagalaz and the Aghori. Namely, that they appear to have been created entirely around the idea of being against the Code of Ananda, with issues about why they are against it or what they're even trying to accomplish secondary considerations. I think there also seems to have been little thought given to the traditions/subcultures those groups were based one. For example, the Aghori are a real religious tradition in Hinduism. Their beliefs are somewhat complex, but in spite of them being contradictory to orthodox Hinduism in a number of ways, they are often held in great reverence by the rural populations they live near. And they don't really bear more than a cursory resemblance to the real world group. I suspect that what you see in even the original tradition book is based on a misunderstanding of what was described in whatever source Phil Brucato encountered them in. In a similar fashion, the Acharne seem to be derived from a very shallow understanding and misinterpretation of the real world 18th century Hellfire Club and on the French Libertine subculture that largely inspired it. (Trying to tie them into unrelated Greek Stoicism only further muddies things.) I'm not at all into S&amp;M and related subcultures, but I've known people who are, and they were more than happy to answer questions about it, so I have long recognized that the Acharne are conceptually broken. <br /> <br /> (TL;DR version of that: The Acharne come across as the creation of someone who things S&amp;M is like what's in 50 Shades of Grey, a book that infuriates actually practitioners of the subculture, because it's actually about an obsessive creep who wants to hurt women who remind him of his mother.)<br /> <br /> The other issue is that all of these rebels against the Code of Ananda don't seem to actually reflect what the Code is. It's not a &quot;sunshine and rainbows&quot; thing, and the Cult has never really shied away from some of the rougher and even darker aspects of human nature. The Nine Sacred Passions include Sadness, Envy, Rage, and Hate as well as Joy, Love, and Empathy, and all are considered equally worthy. The Code itself is entirely in the form of proverbs, most of which are up for interpretation. Even as wonky as the three faction are as presented in both editions of the Tradition book, one can make an argument that they are still following the Code, at least as they interpret it. One of the tenets is &quot;Even trees rent by lightning may grow new fruit&quot;, and someone - say a member of the Hagalaz - could argue that they are the lightning that is making the way for new fruit by destroying certain aspects of modern society (such as Christian belief and morality, and its influence on the culture). Likewise, the bit about transcending fear. Acharne may claim that they're both overcoming their own fear of demons and of hurting other people, and even teaching others to overcome their own fears of being hurt. (And yes, so a degree, this is a probably self indulgent BS. It really only holds up if the people you are teaching come to you freely and of their own desire.)<br /> <br /> (TL;DR: The Code of Ananda is kind of vague and up for interpretation. These groups shouldn't really be rejectors of it but just have their own odd interpretations of it.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-06T21:37:22-07:00","id":1406725,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised?p=1406640#post1406640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thank you. I just don\u2019t like the Acharne at all, though they would make good \u201cevil\u201d NPCs.</div> </div> </div> </div>You may or may not find this version more agreeable.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9</b> (French for &quot;The Relentless Desire&quot;)<br /> <br /> Classifed under the Bringers of Joy, the <i>kamamarga</i> (path of ecstasy) devoted to pleasure and joy as the primary way to reach <i>Lankashim</i>. They are believed to be an offshoot of the Fellowship of Pan, born out of the Libertine movement in 18th century France and the British Hellfire Clubs they helped inspire. While the initial members where all men, they were soon joined by a number of women who proved themselves more than a match for their male counterparts. (It's long rumored that Marianna of Balador was a member at some point, but she has never given a straight answer when asked.) Over the centuries, they have evolved with the times, now being heavily involved in modern S&amp;M/B&amp;D subcultures. This includes a commitment to the ideas of consent and limits. Members restrict more extreme activities to themselves and their willing acolytes, only involving outsiders in lesser activities such as simple sex play and efforts to nudge people outside their (usually non-sexual) comfort zones. <br /> Their mystical beliefs are based on a study of contrasts and opposites. Pleasure and Pain are two sides of the same coin, and both can lead to Lankashim. In fact, they two can often overlap, blurring the boundaries between them. They philosophical underpinnings of the master and slave dynamic also form a key pillar to their world view. As they hew closely to the ideals of S&amp;M subculture, this includes the idea that in a consensual relationship, the submissive is the one who actually holds the real power. And while physical pleasure is their almost obsessive pursuit, it is ultimately done in the quest for spiritual growth and enlightenment.<br /> Members will frequently test each other psychologically and emotionally, in consensual contests of will, interrogation sessions, and elaborate mind games. They do this to better understand their own desires and weaknesses, as well as to push past their own limits. A side effect is that this makes them very effective agents in the Ascension War, able to undertake dangerous missions without fear and resist Technocratic or even Nephandic mental assaults. <br /> One of the more controversial practices of the sect is that of demonology. Members summon and bind lesser (and sometimes more powerful) demons as servants. These bound demons are sometimes used as familiars, but most often to engage in mind games and tests of will. They are also used in the act of sacred congress (ie ritual sex), and it's not uncommon for this to involve an S&amp;M dynamic. (This will frequently involve the mage acting as the submissive, proving their ultimate control over the demon.)<br /> Lust is one of the focuses for the sect, but members also cultivate the passions of Ambition and Wonder. In addition, many also delve into Fear (seeking to understand, master and transcend their own) and Empathy (understanding both sides of the master/slave dynamic and subjecting themselves to the same torments they give to others). <br /> While Time is still the key Sphere for all members of the Cult of Ecstasy, members of L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9 usually strive to master the magic of Mind, Life and Spirit as well. <br /> <br /> <br /> (In the 1970s, an extremist faction of L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9 founded the <i>LeMarchand Club</i> in Los Angeles. Considered part of the Destroyers of the Flesh <i>kamamarga</i>, members no longer differentiate between the sensations of pleasure and pain. They use magic and summoned spirits to subject themselves to tortures that would be otherwise impossible for mundane humans to survive, all in an effort to achieve the ultimate in ecstatic bliss. Thankfully, they don't involve anyone outside the Club in their activities.)<br /> <br /> <br /> While I could probably recommend a number of books as inspiration, I'll just stick to movies.<ul><li>Quills (2000), a pretty decent bio-pic with Geoffrey Rush as the Marquis de Sade.</li> <li>Secretary (2002), one of the better made films examining a master-submissive relationship.</li> <li>The Game (1997), for the sort of mind games they do.</li> <li>Hellraiser (1987), which is entirely where the idea of the LeMarchand Club comes from.</li> </ul><br /> Also the various episodes of CSI where it was used as their &quot;subculture of the week.&quot;<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-07T06:38:39-07:00","id":1406754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1406563-just-read-cult-of-ecstasy-revised?p=1406725#post1406725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You may or may not find this version more agreeable.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9</b> (French for &amp;quot;The Relentless Desire&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Classifed under the Bringers of Joy, the <i>kamamarga</i> (path of ecstasy) devoted to pleasure and joy as the primary way to reach <i>Lankashim</i>. They are believed to be an offshoot of the Fellowship of Pan, born out of the Libertine movement in 18th century France and the British Hellfire Clubs they helped inspire. While the initial members where all men, they were soon joined by a number of women who proved themselves more than a match for their male counterparts. (It's long rumored that Marianna of Balador was a member at some point, but she has never given a straight answer when asked.) Over the centuries, they have evolved with the times, now being heavily involved in modern S&amp;M/B&amp;D subcultures. This includes a commitment to the ideas of consent and limits. Members restrict more extreme activities to themselves and their willing acolytes, only involving outsiders in lesser activities such as simple sex play and efforts to nudge people outside their (usually non-sexual) comfort zones. <br /> Their mystical beliefs are based on a study of contrasts and opposites. Pleasure and Pain are two sides of the same coin, and both can lead to Lankashim. In fact, they two can often overlap, blurring the boundaries between them. They philosophical underpinnings of the master and slave dynamic also form a key pillar to their world view. As they hew closely to the ideals of S&amp;M subculture, this includes the idea that in a consensual relationship, the submissive is the one who actually holds the real power. And while physical pleasure is their almost obsessive pursuit, it is ultimately done in the quest for spiritual growth and enlightenment.<br /> Members will frequently test each other psychologically and emotionally, in consensual contests of will, interrogation sessions, and elaborate mind games. They do this to better understand their own desires and weaknesses, as well as to push past their own limits. A side effect is that this makes them very effective agents in the Ascension War, able to undertake dangerous missions without fear and resist Technocratic or even Nephandic mental assaults. <br /> One of the more controversial practices of the sect is that of demonology. Members summon and bind lesser (and sometimes more powerful) demons as servants. These bound demons are sometimes used as familiars, but most often to engage in mind games and tests of will. They are also used in the act of sacred congress (ie ritual sex), and it's not uncommon for this to involve an S&amp;M dynamic. (This will frequently involve the mage acting as the submissive, proving their ultimate control over the demon.)<br /> Lust is one of the focuses for the sect, but members also cultivate the passions of Ambition and Wonder. In addition, many also delve into Fear (seeking to understand, master and transcend their own) and Empathy (understanding both sides of the master/slave dynamic and subjecting themselves to the same torments they give to others). <br /> While Time is still the key Sphere for all members of the Cult of Ecstasy, members of L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9 usually strive to master the magic of Mind, Life and Spirit as well. <br /> <br /> <br /> (In the 1970s, an extremist faction of L'D\u00e9sir Acharn\u00e9 founded the <i>LeMarchand Club</i> in Los Angeles. Considered part of the Destroyers of the Flesh <i>kamamarga</i>, members no longer differentiate between the sensations of pleasure and pain. They use magic and summoned spirits to subject themselves to tortures that would be otherwise impossible for mundane humans to survive, all in an effort to achieve the ultimate in ecstatic bliss. Thankfully, they don't involve anyone outside the Club in their activities.)<br /> <br /> <br /> While I could probably recommend a number of books as inspiration, I'll just stick to movies.<ul><li>Quills (2000), a pretty decent bio-pic with Geoffrey Rush as the Marquis de Sade.</li> <li>Secretary (2002), one of the better made films examining a master-submissive relationship.</li> <li>The Game (1997), for the sort of mind games they do.</li> <li>Hellraiser (1987), which is entirely where the idea of the LeMarchand Club comes from.</li> </ul><br /> Also the various episodes of CSI where it was used as their &amp;quot;subculture of the week.&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>In something tangentially related , Marquis de Sade was a gar