e as a child, likely at the Jesuit school where he was first introduced to flagellation, by one or more of the priests. <br /> <br /> Most of his work I've tried to look at I've found almost unreadable. It often feels like it's trying to be shocking just for the sake of being shocking. 120 Days of Sodom gets <i>very</i> repetitive <i>very</i> quickly, and thus is boring. (I sometimes wonder if that's intentionally the point, trying to show how quickly shocking behavior becomes normal and boring. I'm probably giving De Sade too much credit there.) The novel Juliette, if nothing else, makes an interesting (if extremely trashy) origin story for a certain type of vampire, Infernalist or Nephandi.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}